preConditionHook:
// A QUALITY MARKER OF A STRONG BULL REVERSAL BAR: the bar opens at or within
// nearTicks ABOVE the prior bar's close -- no gap against the reversal -- and
// closes above both its own open and that prior close, so the whole bar is
// a move up from where the last one ended. Mirrored at a top.
//
// EL: open <= close[1] + nearTicks * tickSize and close > open and
//     close > close[1]
//
// ONE RULE, NOT THREE. The open term alone says nothing (an open near the prior
// close is the ordinary case); it is the pair "started where the last bar ended
// AND finished above it" that is the claim, the same shape as ProfitProtector's
// two readings of one history. Split, neither half is meaningful.
//
// THE THRESHOLD. V00-31 and T00-40 read "near" as one tick, T05-02 as plain
// at-or-below; nearTicks = 0 recovers T05-02 exactly, and 1 is the default
// since 0 would make the input pointless.
//
// Not LowHoldsPriorClose, which tests this bar's *low* against the prior close
// with a price-point tolerance and carries no body terms. Not
// BarLowHoldsPriorClose, the same operands as that one in ticks. Nothing in the
// corpus reads the *opening price* against the prior close.
//
// StrongBullReversalBarComposite inlines this test as quality (1) of its
// m-of-n score at nearTicks = 1; this is the atomic rule it names in
// depends_on_keys.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables
// entry, never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double
// placement. Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
